steps.body:
Some layouts have to line up in two directions at once: a page with a header across the top, a sidebar, and the main content, or a gallery of cards in neat columns. Flexbox lays out one line at a time, so its rows don't line up with each other. **CSS Grid** draws the rows and columns first, then puts each box in its place on them.

## Making a grid

`display: grid` makes a **grid container**, and its direct children become **grid items**. `grid-template-columns` lists the columns, one size each:

```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

That's three columns: one 16rem wide, then two that share the rest. The **fr** unit means a fraction of the free space, what's left after the fixed sizes and the gaps. `1fr 1fr` splits it equally, and `2fr 1fr` gives the first column two thirds and the second one third.

The items fill the cells in order, left to right along the first row, then the next. You don't have to list the rows: the grid adds a new row whenever the items need one, each as tall as its tallest item. `grid-template-rows` sets rows when you want to, and `grid-auto-rows` sets the size of the ones the grid adds.

## Columns that fit the screen

Three helpers make column lists short and flexible:

- `repeat(4, 1fr)` is the same as `1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr`.
- `minmax(12rem, 1fr)` is a size with a floor and a ceiling: never narrower than 12rem, never wider than one share.
- `auto-fit` in place of the number asks the browser to fit as many columns as it can.

Put together, they make a grid that changes its number of columns by itself:

```css
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

Every column is at least 15rem, and as many fit as the width allows, each stretched to fill the row. A phone gets one column, a tablet two, and a wide screen four, and you wrote no rule for any of them. `auto-fill` does almost the same, but keeps empty columns when there are only a few items, where `auto-fit` lets the items stretch across the whole row.

## Lines, and placing items

The lines between the columns and rows are numbered, starting at 1 on the starting edge. Three columns have four column lines, and negative numbers count from the far end, so -1 is always the last line. You place an item by the lines it runs between:

- `grid-column: 1 / 3` runs from line 1 to line 3, across two columns.
- `grid-column: 1 / -1` runs the whole width, however many columns there are.
- `grid-column: span 2` takes two columns, starting wherever it lands.

## Named areas

Line numbers get hard to read in a whole page layout, so you can draw the layout with names instead. `grid-template-areas` takes one string per row, with one name per cell:

```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr 1fr;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header  header header"
    "sidebar main   main"
    "footer  footer footer";
}
```

The same name in neighboring cells makes one area across them, as long as the area is a rectangle, and a `.` leaves a cell empty. Then each item says which area it goes in, like `grid-area: header`.

```diagram flow-flexbox-and-grid-grid-lines
Three columns make four numbered lines. The header area runs from line 1 to line 4, and main from line 2 to line 4.
```

In the diagram, the header spans all three columns, the sidebar fills the first column of the middle row, and main spans the other two. The numbers along the top are the column lines: the header runs from 1 to 4, which is the same as `grid-column: 1 / -1`.

## Subgrid

Cards in a row often have three parts, a title, some text, and a button, and the titles aren't all the same length. Each card lays out its own parts, so the buttons end up at different heights. `grid-template-rows: subgrid` on a card that spans three rows of the outer grid makes its parts use the outer grid's rows instead. Now every title, every text, and every button sits on the same rows across all the cards. Subgrid works in every current browser since 2023.

examples.code:
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header  header"
    "sidebar main"
    "footer  footer";
  gap: 1rem;
  min-height: 100dvh;
}

.page > header { grid-area: header; }
.page > aside  { grid-area: sidebar; }
.page > main   { grid-area: main; }
.page > footer { grid-area: footer; }

.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(15rem, 100%), 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(15rem, 100%), 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

.card {
  display: grid;
  grid-row: span 3;
  grid-template-rows: subgrid;
  gap: 0.5rem;
}

body {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
  min-height: 100dvh;
  margin: 0;
}
